course: biology
lesson: 298
title: The big biology quiz, part 1: habitats and animals
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: This is the first of three big quizzes at the end of the course, like the last day of term when the class plays a quiz and every team remembers what it learned.
explain: Part one is about animals and where they live: the biggest cat, the fastest flier, the longest traveller, the animal with three hearts, and why polar bears and penguins never meet.
explain: Many answers come from one big idea: every animal's body fits its home and its way of life. A camel's wide feet fit the sand, an octopus's spare hearts fit life in the sea, and a dormouse's long winter sleep fits a cold season with no food.
explain: Cartoons often mix up homes, putting a polar bear and a penguin on the same iceberg, so a good quiz player always asks: north or south, land or sea, day or night?
fact: The tiger is the biggest cat, especially the Siberian tiger; the lion is second, and the jaguar is the biggest cat in the Americas, while the cheetah is the fastest.
fact: Polar bears live in the Arctic in the far north, and penguins live mostly in the south, around Antarctica and in southern seas, so they never meet in the wild; the Galapagos penguin lives nearest the equator.
fact: Whales are mammals, not fish: they breathe air, have warm blood, feed their babies milk and even have a few hairs, while sharks are fish.
fact: Spiders have eight legs and two body parts, insects have six legs and three body parts; spiders belong to the arachnids, with scorpions and ticks.
fact: The Arctic tern has the longest migration, flying from the Arctic to the Antarctic and back each year, about 70000 kilometres, following the summer from one end of the Earth to the other, so it sees more daylight than any other animal.
fact: The peregrine falcon is the fastest animal, diving at over 300 kilometres an hour; on land the cheetah reaches about 100 to 110, and in water the sailfish is among the fastest.
fact: Camels can go days without water; the hump stores fat, not water, their bodies lose little water, long eyelashes and closable nostrils keep sand out, and wide feet do not sink.
fact: Herbivores eat plants, carnivores eat meat and omnivores eat both: a cow is a herbivore, a cat a carnivore, and bears and people are omnivores, and teeth show what an animal eats: meat-eaters have sharp cutting teeth, plant-eaters flat grinding ones.
fact: Camouflage is colours and shapes that help an animal hide, like a polar bear in snow or a leaf insect on a twig; some animals, like wasps, do the opposite and show bright warning colours.
fact: The octopus has three hearts and blue blood: two hearts pump blood through the gills and one through the body.
fact: Some animals hibernate through winter when food is scarce, slowing their heart and breathing to save energy; hedgehogs, dormice and many bats hibernate, while bears sleep deeply but less deeply than true hibernators.
fact: In the rainforest the canopy, the leafy roof of the forest, holds the most animals, because it gets the most sun and fruit; many canopy animals never touch the ground.
exercise: Which is the biggest cat in the world? | The tiger | The African lion | The jaguar | The cheetah
exercise: Where do polar bears live? | In the Arctic, in the far north | In Antarctica, in the far south | On both poles at once | In mountain forests of Africa
exercise: Where do most penguins live? | Around Antarctica and in southern seas | In the Arctic, on the ice with the polar bears | On high mountains in the far north | In rivers of Europe
exercise: Is a whale a fish? | No, it is a mammal that breathes air and feeds its babies milk | Yes, because it lives its whole life in the sea and never comes onto land | Yes, because it has fins and a tail | No, it is a giant sea reptile, like a turtle
exercise: Is a spider an insect? | No, it has eight legs and two body parts | Yes, every small creepy-crawly is an insect | Yes, it has six legs like an ant | No, it is a kind of worm
exercise: Which animal has the longest migration? | The Arctic tern | The barn swallow | The African elephant | The Atlantic salmon
exercise: Which is the fastest animal of all? | The peregrine falcon in a dive | The cheetah running on open land | The sailfish in the sea | The racehorse on a track at full gallop
exercise: What is inside a camel's hump? | Fat | Water | Hot air | Spare bones
exercise: What is an omnivore? | An animal that eats both plants and meat | An animal that eats only plants | An animal that eats only meat | An animal that eats only insects
exercise: Which of these is a carnivore? | A cat | A dairy cow | A bear | A rabbit
exercise: What is camouflage? | Colours and shapes that help an animal hide | Bright colours that warn hungry enemies to stay away | A loud call that scares hunters | Sharp spikes that hurt an attacker
exercise: Which animal has three hearts? | The octopus | The blue whale | The great white shark | The hermit crab
exercise: Why do some animals hibernate? | To save energy when winter food is scarce | Because they are lazy in winter and do not want to work | To grow bigger while asleep | Because the cold freezes them solid until spring
exercise: Which of these animals truly hibernates? | The dormouse | The red fox | The robin | The red deer of the hills
exercise: Which rainforest layer has the most animals? | The canopy, the leafy roof | The forest floor | The roots under the ground | The rivers alone
exercise: Why does the canopy hold so much life? | It gets the most sun and fruit | It is the coldest layer | It is the darkest place to hide | It is closest to the rivers
test: In a Christmas advert a polar bear gives a present to a penguin on an iceberg. What is wrong? | Polar bears live in the north and penguins in the south, so they never meet | Penguins are far too small to hold a present with their flippers | Polar bears live only in zoos | Nothing, they share the same ice
test: «whale's a fish innit, lives in the sea, got fins» What is right? | No, it breathes air, has warm blood and feeds its babies milk: a mammal | Yes, anything with fins and a tail that swims in the sea is a fish | Yes, and so is a dolphin | No, a whale is an amphibian
test: Count them: 3 spiders and 2 ants are on the window. How many legs altogether? | 36 | 30 | 40 | 24
test: Count them: 5 octopuses swim past the boat. How many hearts do they have together? | 15 | 5 | 10 | 40
test: Who would win a race across land, sea and air: a cheetah, a sailfish or a diving peregrine falcon? | The peregrine falcon, at over 300 kilometres an hour | The cheetah, because it is the fastest animal of all | The sailfish, because water is fast | All three tie at exactly the same speed
test: «camels store water in the hump, everybody knows that» What is the best correction? | The hump stores fat; camels save water by losing very little | Right, the hump is a water tank that sloshes as it walks | The hump is full of air for floating | The hump stores sand
test: Why does the Arctic tern see more daylight than any other animal? | It follows the summer from the far north to the far south | It never sleeps at all, not even for a minute | It flies only at midday | It lives on the equator, where the sun is strongest all year
test: A wasp is bright yellow and black. Why? | It is a warning colour that says «I sting» | It is camouflage among green leaves | It helps it hide in the dark | It keeps the wasp cool
test: In a cartoon a lion eats grass with a cow. What do the teeth of a real lion tell you? | Sharp meat-cutting teeth show it is a carnivore | Flat grinding teeth show it is a plant-eater, like the cow | Lions have no teeth at all | Its teeth show it eats only fruit
test: «hedgehog's gone all cold and slow in the leaf pile, is it dead??» What is most likely? | It is hibernating, with its heart and breathing slowed down | It is dead from the cold and should be taken out at once | It is pretending for fun | It has turned into a ball of leaves
test: In a cartoon a sloth hangs from the treetops and never comes down. In which layer does it live? | The canopy | The forest floor | The tree roots | The river bed
test: Which penguin lives nearest the equator? | The Galapagos penguin | The emperor penguin | The king penguin | No penguin goes north of Antarctica
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the classification of whales. | Whales are mammals, which respire air and nourish their young with milk | Whales are fish, by reason of their fins | Whales are reptiles inhabiting the ocean | Whales are amphibians capable of residence on land
exam: Identify the principal distinction between spiders and insects. | Spiders possess eight legs and two body parts, insects six legs and three | Spiders possess six legs and three body parts, insects eight legs and two | Spiders possess wings, insects possess none | Spiders are vertebrates, insects are invertebrates
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the function of the camel's hump? | It constitutes a store of fat | It constitutes a reservoir of water | It contains air for the purpose of flotation | It contains a supplementary stomach
exam: State the number of hearts possessed by an octopus, together with the colour of its blood. | Three hearts and blue blood | One heart and red blood | Two hearts and green blood | Eight hearts and colourless blood
